{"kind":"report","text":"DEPLOYMENT ROUND 12 CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 2422266cf2016789e3b479099508495f2dbfcf39, against review request a330963ace355c26bfe5c6f9eb461e8980a3725b and promise 16e64da40b5f59ef49256d3b27fd580a4a29d203. The five round-11 repairs are substantively present: case 4 releases the claim before pruning and requires ref enumeration; the all-refused sentence retains candidate parents; candidate= now names Git objects; refusal replay names descriptor/P/profile inputs; and the first ledger creation has an absent-value lease. Probe /tmp/gitseq-deployment-r12.GmJp2K confirms the lease mechanism: first absent-root creation succeeds, a stale second creation fails, and atomicity prevents its side ref from landing. Line order being insignificant does not conflict with sequence order because admitted lines name event commits.\n\nThree blockers remain. First, the ledger payload schema does not encode two values later treated as recorded: the shown format has only identity, candidate, result, and event/reason, but replay requires the entry's admission-profile version and recorded P. The released claim was the object that recorded P and is not a ledger parent, so after claim deletion it can be pruned; in an all-refused first batch the ledger also omits E=P and has no admitted event from which P can be derived. Add explicit content-addressed profile identity and P (or an equally mechanical derivation) to the retained ledger entry. Recording an opaque version string is insufficient: name where historical profile rules come from and state the retention obligation that lets a fresh auditor execute them.\n\nSecond, duplicate candidate objects make the advertised bijection impossible as written. The submitter credential can create a canonical inbox ref and an alias pointing to the same commit; the probe created both. The drain then considered two submissions, while Git commit-tree reports 'duplicate parent ... ignored' and stores the candidate only once. Two payload lines violate the repeated-candidate fault rule; one line violates 'every submission exactly once.' Specify normalization and terminal treatment for alias/duplicate refs, and test canonical+alias and two-alias cases with a positive control.\n\nThird, ledger traversal still lacks a codeable root boundary and parent-role convention. The first entry has E as its first parent, while later entries have a ledger predecessor; a plain first-parent walk from a constructed first entry continued into E and its history rather than terminating. Specify ordered parent roles plus an explicit first-entry marker/root condition (and how an auditor distinguishes predecessor, E, and candidate parents). The same schema should make first versus later arity mechanically validatable.\n\nMinor alignment: the actual acquisition transaction already deletes inbox refs, so case 4 should assert they are absent after claim release rather than describe deleting them then, unless it explicitly says the test fixture recreates them. Diff check passes. Approval remains exact-head only.","body":{"head":"2422266cf2016789e3b479099508495f2dbfcf39","probe":"/tmp/gitseq-deployment-r12.GmJp2K","verdict":"changes-requested"}}